# Ch3 - Self Attention

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:gpt3_tokenizer, "~> 0.1.1"},
  {:nx, "~> 0.10.0"},
  {:exla, "~> 0.10.0"}
])
Nx.global_default_backend(EXLA.Backend)
```

## Self Attention without training weights

A small, learning-oriented implementation of self-attention written in Elixir while working through Chapter 3 of *Build a Large Language Model (from Scratch)*. The goal here is clarity and intuition rather than completeness or performance. For a full explanation of the concepts and design decisions, see the accompanying blog post: [https://latentwords.com](https://www.latentwords.com/understanding-self-attention-in-language-models/)

Before introducing the full transformer attention mechanism, it helps to start with a simplified version. In this module we compute self-attention without any trainable weights, using the embedding vectors directly. This lets us focus on the core mechanics of attention.

```elixir
defmodule SelfAttention do
  import Nx.Defn

  # Randomly initialize embedding vectors for a given vocabulary size and embedding dimension
   def init_embedding({rows, cols}, seed \\ 0) do
    {embeddings, _key} =
      Nx.Random.uniform(
        Nx.Random.key(seed),
        0.0,
        1.0,
        shape: {rows, cols},
        type: {:f, 32}
      )

    embeddings
  end

  # Replace input token IDs with their embedding vectors
  def embed(token_ids, token_embedding) do
    Nx.take(token_embedding, token_ids)
  end

  # Step 1: Each row is the unnormalized scores for token i attending to every other token
  def attention_scores(embeddings) do
    Nx.dot(embeddings, Nx.transpose(embeddings))
  end

  # Step 2: Normalize each row 
  defn softmax(scores) do
    max = Nx.reduce_max(scores, axes: [1], keep_axes: true)
    exp = Nx.exp(scores - max)
    exp / Nx.sum(exp, axes: [1], keep_axes: true)
  end

  # Step 3: Each row i of the result is the context vector for token i
  def context_vectors(embeddings, attn_weights) do
    Nx.dot(attn_weights, embeddings)
  end
end
```

First let's get the tokens and embeddings.

```elixir
text = "Your journey starts with one step"
tokens = Gpt3Tokenizer.encode(text)

vocab_size = Enum.max(tokens) + 1
embedding_dim = 8
token_embeddings = SelfAttention.init_embedding({vocab_size, embedding_dim})
token_ids = Nx.tensor(tokens)
embeddings = SelfAttention.embed(token_ids, token_embeddings)
:ok
```

The next step is to compute similarity scores between every pair of words in the sentence. We do this by taking the dot product between each word’s embedding vector and the embedding vectors of all other words. The goal is to measure how closely related each word might be to the others in this specific context.

Once we have those scores, we normalize them with softmax so each word produces a set of attention weights. Finally, we use those weights to compute context vectors, weighted combinations of the input embeddings that blend information from the most relevant words in the sentence.

```elixir
attn_scores = SelfAttention.attention_scores(embeddings)
attn_weights = SelfAttention.softmax(attn_scores)
context = SelfAttention.context_vectors(embeddings, attn_weights)
IO.inspect(context, label: "Context vectors [seq_len x emb_dim]")
:ok
```

## Trainable Self Attention with Query, Key and Value Matrix

The simplified version above helps explain the mechanics of attention, but real transformers do not compare the raw embeddings directly. Instead, they learn three separate linear projections that transform the input embeddings into queries (Q), keys (K), and values (V).

Because the surrounding mechanics are the same, this module reuses the earlier embedding initialization and softmax functions and focuses only on the new trainable pieces.

```elixir
defmodule SelfAttentionWithTrainableWeights do
  import Nx.Defn

  def init_weights({d_in, d_out}, seed) do
    limit = :math.sqrt(6.0 / (d_in + d_out))

    {weights, _key} =
      Nx.Random.uniform(
        Nx.Random.key(seed),
        -limit,
        limit,
        shape: {d_in, d_out},
        type: {:f, 32}
      )

    weights
  end

  defn attention(x, w_q, w_k, w_v) do
    q = Nx.dot(x, w_q)
    k = Nx.dot(x, w_k)
    v = Nx.dot(x, w_v)

    scale = Nx.sqrt(Nx.axis_size(k, 1))
    scores = Nx.dot(q, Nx.transpose(k)) / scale

    weights = SelfAttention.softmax(scores)
    context = Nx.dot(weights, v)

    {weights, context}
  end

end
:ok
```

`d_out` defines the size of those projected vectors (the attention head dimension). We initialize `w_q`, `w_k`, and `w_v`, then pass them along with the embeddings into the attention function to compute the attention weights and resulting context vectors. In a real model these weights would be updated during training by an optimizer.

```elixir
d_out = 4  # head / projection dimension (can differ from embedding_dim)

# Trainable weight matrices — in a real model these are updated by the optimizer
w_q = SelfAttentionWithTrainableWeights.init_weights({embedding_dim, d_out}, 1)
w_k = SelfAttentionWithTrainableWeights.init_weights({embedding_dim, d_out}, 2)
w_v = SelfAttentionWithTrainableWeights.init_weights({embedding_dim, d_out}, 3)

{attn_weights, context} = SelfAttentionWithTrainableWeights.attention(embeddings, w_q, w_k, w_v)
IO.inspect(attn_weights, label: "Attention weights [seq_len x seq_len]")
IO.inspect(context, label: "Context vectors [seq_len x d_out]")
:ok
```
